meta {
  name: Space Get Latest Commit
  type: http
  seq: 147
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/xrpc/com.atproto.space.getLatestCommit?space=at://did:plc:authorityaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/space/org.example.bucket/main&repo={{did}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

params:query {
  space: at://did:plc:authorityaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/space/org.example.bucket/main
  repo: {{did}}
}

docs {
  The current signed commit for a permissioned repo. It is MINTED PER SERVING, never stored:
  each call draws a fresh "ikm", signs the (space, author, rev, ikm) context, and MACs the
  repo hash under a key derived from that ikm. Two calls over an unchanged repo therefore
  return the same "hash" and "rev" but different "ikm"/"sig"/"mac" — that is not a bug.

  The signature deliberately does not cover the hash, so a leaked commit proves only that the
  user signed a context: anyone holding its ikm can forge a matching mac for any hash. That
  deniability is the point.

  "hash", "ikm", "sig" and "mac" are lex-JSON byte strings ({"$bytes": "<base64>"}). A repo
  the account has never written to returns 400 RepoNotFound.
}
